.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt OSSL-GUIDE-INTRODUCTION 7ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ossl-guide-introduction
.Nd OpenSSL Guide: \&An introduction to OpenSSL
.Sh WHAT IS OPENSSL?
OpenSSL is a robust, commercial-grade, full-featured toolkit for
general-purpose cryptography and secure communication.
Its features are made available via a command line application that
enables users to perform various cryptography related functions such as
generating keys and certificates.
Additionally it supplies two libraries that application developers can
use to implement cryptography based capabilities and to securely
communicate across a network.
Finally, it also has a set of providers that supply implementations of a
broad set of cryptographic algorithms.
.Pp
OpenSSL is fully open source.
Version 3.0 and above are distributed under the Apache v2 license.
.Sh GETTING AND INSTALLING OPENSSL
The OpenSSL Project develops and distributes the source code for
OpenSSL. You can obtain that source code via the OpenSSL website
.Pf ( Lk https://www.openssl.org/source Ns ).
.Pp
Many Operating Systems (notably Linux distributions) supply pre-built
OpenSSL binaries either pre-installed or available via the package
management system in use for that OS. It is worth checking whether this
applies to you before attempting to build OpenSSL from the source code.
.Pp
Some third parties also supply OpenSSL binaries (e.g.
for Windows and some other platforms).
The OpenSSL project maintains a list of these third parties at
.Lk https://github.com/openssl/openssl/wiki/Binaries .
.Pp
If you build and install OpenSSL from the source code then you should
download the appropriate files for the version that you want to use from
the link given above.
Extract the contents of the
.Sy tar.gz
archive file that you downloaded into an appropriate directory.
Inside that archive you will find a file named
.Sy INSTALL.md
which will supply detailed instructions on how to build and install
OpenSSL from source.
Make sure you read the contents of that file carefully in order to
achieve a successful build.
In the directory you will also find a set of
.Sy NOTES
files that provide further platform specific information.
Make sure you carefully read the file appropriate to your platform.
As well as the platform specific
.Sy NOTES
files there is also a
.Sy NOTES-PERL.md
file that provides information about setting up Perl for use by the
OpenSSL build system across multiple platforms.
.Pp
Sometimes you may want to build and install OpenSSL from source on a
system which already has a pre-built version of OpenSSL installed on it
via the Operating System package management system (for example if you
want to use a newer version of OpenSSL than the one supplied by your
Operating System).
In this case it is strongly recommended to install OpenSSL to a
different location than where the pre-built version is installed.
You should
.Sy never
replace the pre-built version with a different version as this may break
your system.
.Sh CONTENTS OF THE OPENSSL GUIDE
The OpenSSL Guide is a series of documentation pages (starting with this
one) that introduce some of the main concepts in OpenSSL. The guide can
either be read end-to-end in order, or alternatively you can simply skip
to the parts most applicable to your use case.
Note however that later pages may depend on and assume knowledge from
earlier pages.
.Pp
The pages in the guide are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Xr ossl-guide-libraries-introduction 7 Ns : \&An introduction to the OpenSSL libraries
.It Xr ossl-guide-libcrypto-introduction 7 Ns : \&An introduction to libcrypto
.It Xr ossl-guide-libssl-introduction 7 Ns : \&An introduction to libssl
.It Xr ossl-guide-tls-introduction 7 Ns : \&An introduction to SSL/TLS in OpenSSL
.It Xr ossl-guide-tls-client-block 7 Ns : Writing a simple blocking TLS client
.It Xr ossl-guide-tls-client-non-block 7 Ns : Writing a simple nonblocking TLS client
.It Xr ossl-guide-tls-server-block 7 Ns : Writing a simple blocking TLS server
.It Xr ossl-guide-quic-introduction 7 Ns : \&An introduction to QUIC in OpenSSL
.It Xr ossl-guide-quic-client-block 7 Ns : Writing a simple blocking QUIC client
.It Xr ossl-guide-quic-server-block 7 Ns : Writing a simple blocking QUIC server
.It Xr ossl-guide-quic-multi-stream 7 Ns : Writing a simple multi-stream QUIC client
.It Xr ossl-guide-quic-server-non-block 7 Ns : Writing a simple nonblocking QUIC server
.It Xr ossl-guide-quic-client-non-block 7 Ns : Writing a simple nonblocking QUIC client
.It Xr ossl-guide-migration 7 Ns : Migrating from older OpenSSL versions
